#original-page(612pt, 792pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(233.81pt, 30.96pt, "Submitted Manuscript: Confidential ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#4f81bd"))
  #line-at(303.53pt, 742.416pt, "9 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(96.024pt, 75.96pt, "To ensure both flexibility and performance of the heat dissipation film, we fabricated two ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 89.78pt, "types of flexible heat dissipation films with excellent thermal conductivity for comparison: an ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 103.58pt, "anisotropic film and an isotropic film. These were made by using boron nitride and the liquid metal ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 117.38pt, "composite material reported in our previous work ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(318.41pt, 117.38pt, "(70, 71) ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(360.91pt, 117.38pt, "(Fig. 4a, Fig. S21), respectively, and ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 131.18pt, "we tested their bending performance (Fig. 4b). Non-reciprocal heat transfer devices were ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 144.98pt, "fabricated using these two flexible heat dissipation thin films. The results show that the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 158.78pt, "performance difference between them is within 5% (Fig. S22), indicating that in-plane thermal ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 172.58pt, "conductivity plays a dominant role in heat dissipation. To verify the practical performance of the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 186.38pt, "device, we tested the cooling performance of a single-unit device under the optimal structure, with ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 200.18pt, "its dimensions shown in Table 3. The infrared temperature distribution reveals that the heat transfer ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 213.98pt, "between the cold and hot ends is basically blocked (Fig. 4c), and this phenomenon is more obvious ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 227.78pt, "at low current. At an input current of 2.1 A, the temperature difference ∆T of the device reaches ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 241.58pt, "49.95 ℃, with the cold end temperature and temperature drop relative to the environment being -")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 255.41pt, "7.03 ℃ and 29.25 ℃, respectively. This demonstrates excellent cooling performance, covering ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 269.21pt, "wide applications ranging from traditional personal thermal management to surgical-grade ice caps ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 283.01pt, "(Fig. 4d), making it competitive with existing flexible thermoelectric devices reported so far. The ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 296.81pt, "variation of input current alters the maximum attainable temperature difference. At an input current ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 310.61pt, "of 3.6 A, ∆T can reach 64.54 K (Fig. 4e). When the input current direction is reversed, the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 324.41pt, "asymmetric structure leads to asymmetric heat loss, resulting in a significantly higher temperature ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 338.21pt, "at the hot side under the same current magnitude (Fig. 4g). This further expands the potential range ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 352.01pt, "for both cooling and heating applications. ")
  #line-at(96.024pt, 365.81pt, "In current studies, when the temperature difference between the cold and hot ends of a ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 379.61pt, "thermoelectric cooler reaches 25-30 K, its coefficient of performance (COP) is approximately 0.5-")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 393.41pt, "0.7 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(89.784pt, 393.41pt, "(72)")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(109.7pt, 393.41pt, ". Non-reciprocal heat transfer reduces the energy loss of the device caused by internal heat ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 407.21pt, "flow, so the traditional COP calculation method for thermoelectric coolers cannot meet the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 421.01pt, "requirements of the new device. Therefore, we calculated the COP based on energy balance. We ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 434.83pt, "tested the device under the optimal COP structure (its dimensions shown in Table 3), and the COP ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 448.63pt, "can reach approximately 1.5 at a temperature difference of 29 K (Fig. 4f), with excellent cooling ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 462.43pt, "performance of 5.42 ℃ (Fig. S23). ")
  #line-at(96.024pt, 476.23pt, "Meanwhile, we tested the power generation performance of the 7-unit device worn on the arm ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 490.03pt, "during standing and walking, achieving potential differences of approximately 15.6 mV and 26.1 ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 503.83pt, "mV, respectively (Fig. 4h). The power curves of a 2-unit device under different wind speeds were ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 517.63pt, "also measured (Fig. 4i) to demonstrate its power generation capability in various environments. ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 531.43pt, "The designed device no longer requires complex heat sinks, further improving its portability, ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 545.23pt, "reliability, and practicality. Overall, the outstanding performance, relatively low manufacturing ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 559.03pt, "cost, good flexibility, and excellent portability all together confirm the strong potential of the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 572.83pt, "fabricated flexible device in practical applications. ")
]

